# Define macros here
# Macros can be defined in any .defines file inside the ANSL source folder
# Macros can be used in any ANSL script to speed up scripting
# When compiling, the ANSL compiler will replace calls to macros with the macros content 

# Define macros like this
# Define MacroName(Parameter1;Parameter2;...)
# To use a parameter inside a macro, use {ParameterName}

# Example :
# Define UselessTimer(Time)
# wait({Time})
# log(Waited for {Time};Log)
# EndDefine
#
# In this case, calling the macro will copy its content (wait & print) to the required spot in the script

# To call a macro in an ANSL script, use :, followed by its name, and specify the parameters if needed
# Example :       :UselessTimer(5)
# Be mindful of parameter types, macros will not check anything on their own.
# Also, macros have priority over functions. Don't call a macro with an existing function name or you will not be able to call the function anymore.
# Don't call a macro inside the same macro. This will not end well.

Define DebugMacro(Name)
log(Hello from {Name};Log)
wait(2)
EndDefine

Define DebugMacro2()
log(Hello from Mr.Caribou;Log)
wait(1)
EndDefine